window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-f623bd5f7cfb4146"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"investigate-an-incident-and-test-a-fix\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">调查事故并测试修复方案</h1> <p>将事故资料包转化为可复现的缺陷、聚焦的补丁和可供审核的交接材料。</p> <p>为 Codex Cloud 提供经过脱敏的事故资料包和可用的测试环境。调查证据、复现有依据支持的代码缺陷，并交付聚焦的补丁、回归测试证据以及部署和回滚交接材料。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>调查已稳定事故的小型工程团队。</li> <li>拥有脱敏证据和安全复现输入的应用缺陷。</li> <li>希望在可用且隔离的测试环境中审核聚焦修复方案的团队。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">起步提示词</h2> <p>调查一起事故</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">调查 [incident ID]（位于 [repository/service]），使用 [path to sanitized incident packet] 中的资料。在为此任务选定的已发布云端环境中工作。\n\n资料包会说明事故时间范围、受影响版本的 SHA、预期行为、实际观察到的行为、相关日志和跟踪片段，以及安全的复现输入。首先阅读 AGENTS.md 和代码库中的设置及测试说明。确认当前检出的代码与受影响版本一致；如果不一致，应在得出结论前说明差异。\n\n根据资料包和源代码中的文件及行号引用，整理一条简短时间线。将观察事实、假设和证据缺口分开记录。在隔离测试环境中验证最有力的假设。如果无法复现故障或缺少决定性证据，就交付调查结果和下一步诊断建议；不要声称已确认根因，也不要提交推测性修复。\n\n如果证据支持存在代码缺陷，请添加回归测试，并先在原始代码上运行，证明测试会失败。做最小范围的修复，再次运行该测试，然后运行代码库中可用且相关的更广泛测试、lint 和类型检查。记录命令、退出码，以及任何无法运行的检查。\n\n准备分支差异、草稿拉取请求说明、test-evidence.md，以及部署和回滚交接材料。写明有证据支持的原因、尚未解决的不确定性和发生变化的行为。每项验收标准使用一行证据记录：修订版本和环境版本、基线命令/退出码/日志、最终命令/退出码/日志、实际结果，以及附带限制的通过/失败/受阻/未运行状态。将 PR 说明保留为本地草稿以供审核。\n\n对于这个重复 Webhook 示例，指出提交前和提交后的故障点；使用独立数据库连接证明重复请求之间的竞争；并说明隔离级别、回执保留期限和混合版本部署的假设。\n\n对于这个重复 Webhook 示例，在建议恢复处理保留的投递之前，先找出旧版消费者全部停止后仍没有匹配回执的历史订单。使用现有队列或隔离流程暂扣受影响的事件键。添加一个带有待重试事件的孤立记录测试，证明事件被暂扣期间不会创建第二笔订单，也不会发送成功确认。仅依据可信的不可变事件身份、规范化载荷和已提交订单证据进行对账；缺失或冲突的证据应继续隔离，等待负责人审核。不要虚构指纹、删除回执或重放尚未查明的旧事件。\n\n除非我指出其他获准来源，否则只使用提供的资料包和代码库。若提供了明确批准的只读监控来源，只能访问该来源。代码和任务文件只能写入此代码库；只能使用其隔离测试数据库。不要直接连接生产应用或数据库主机，也不要修改生产系统。不要重放生产事件、修改线上数据或监控、创建或发布外部更新、合并或部署。\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">云端环境</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>如需查看完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面的 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>在准备好的云端环境中，给 Codex 一个范围明确的事故进行调查。目标是交付工程师能够审核的结果：有证据支持的解释、一项修复前会失败的回归测试、一份聚焦补丁，以及实际运行测试的证据。</p> <p>事故刚刚稳定下来、后续工作是理解并修复应用缺陷时，这种方式很有用。如果现有证据无法确定原因，那么清楚说明调查过程和下一步诊断建议，也是一项有价值的成果。</p> <p><a id=\"prepare-the-environment-and-incident-packet\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">准备环境和事故资料包</h2> <ol> <li> <p>在网页或桌面应用中，选择 <strong>Work in</strong> &gt; <strong>Cloud</strong>，然后选择一个已发布的环境。</p> </li> <li> <p>若要创建环境，请选择 <strong>Create environment</strong>，选择 GitHub 代码库，并让 Codex 准备运行时、依赖项和隔离的测试服务。</p> </li> <li> <p>检查设置报告、解决失败的检查，并在开始新任务前选择 <strong>Publish</strong>。</p> </li> <li> <p>请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">当前云端环境指南</a>。</p> </li> <li>请将代码库指引保存在 <a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">AGENTS.md</a> 中。</li> </ol> <p>如果依赖项或服务设置发生变化，请编辑可复用的环境、测试并选择 <strong>Republish</strong>，然后在一个新任务中验证更新后的环境。</p> <p>刷新代码库不会重新运行安装或启动命令，现有任务仍会保留各自的状态。</p> <p>请单独配置所需的网络目标和测试凭据；允许访问某个目标，并不意味着已经获得该服务的访问权限。</p> <p>确认受影响的代码检出、依赖项、检查项和一次性测试数据库都能在新任务中正常工作。记录起始提交和测试基线。</p> <p>让初步调查所需的信息集中在一处。将经过脱敏的资料包放入代码库，或以其他方式提供给任务。工程师应在开始之前整理并检查资料包：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>输入</th> <th>应包含的内容</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>事故摘要</td> <td>事故 ID、UTC 时间范围、预期行为和实际观察到的行为、已知影响及当前状态。区分已确认数量和估算数量。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>发布版本身份</td> <td>已部署的提交 SHA、部署时间、不含密钥的相关配置值，以及可用时最后一个已知正常的发布版本。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>关联证据</td> <td>带时间戳、事件 ID、请求 ID、跟踪 ID 和文件引用的日志与跟踪片段。脱敏时应一致地保留这些标识符。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>安全复现方式</td> <td>测试请求正文、相关夹具，以及不调用线上服务即可执行疑似故障路径的说明。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>代码库指引</td> <td>相关路径、数据库引擎、测试命令、允许的更改，以及审核结果的工程师。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>不要在资料包中包含访问令牌、客户载荷或个人数据。</p> <p>监控集成为可选项。如果使用，应授予其对相关项目和时间范围的只读访问权限；基于文件的工作流不需要它。代码库指令应确保测试凭据与生产配置分开。</p> <p><a id=\"run-the-starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">运行起始提示词</h2> <p>使用本页的起始提示词，并将占位符替换为事故 ID、代码库和资料包路径。第一轮结果应把证据与受影响代码联系起来，说明哪些情况可以复现，并指出缺少的信息。</p> <p>只有当证据支持代码缺陷时，才要求 Codex 继续实施修复。否则，应先审核调查结果并补充缺失输入。有说服力的解释、最近发生的代码更改，或在不同发布版本上通过的测试，都不足以确认根因。</p> <p><a id=\"example-a-retried-webhook-creates-a-duplicate-order\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"webhook\">示例：重试的 Webhook 创建了重复订单</h2> <p>使用<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/use-cases/incident-to-tested-fix/sample.zip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">可运行的事故示例（需联网）</a>练习调查。示例包含已准备好的事故资料包、存在可复现缺陷的处理器、修正后的处理器和 PostgreSQL 测试。</p> <p>服务收到 <code>order.created</code> Webhook 后，会写入订单并记录该事件已处理。事故期间，一次重试似乎创建了第二笔订单。团队已经稳定服务，现在希望准备一份可供审核的补丁。</p> <p>示例使用 JavaScript、Node.js 测试运行器和 PostgreSQL。一次性测试夹具包含 <code>event_receipts</code> 表，其身份列不允许为 null，并在 <code>(provider, account_id, event_id)</code> 上设置不可延迟的唯一键。</p> <p>该模式允许在订单创建之前先申领回执，并保留规范化载荷指纹，以便检测同一个事件键被冲突数据重复使用的情况。</p> <p>如果你的应用要求申领时订单外键必须已存在，或缺少这类冲突证据，应停止操作，并单独决定模式/设计方案。</p> <p>在证据中记录确切的运行时、依赖锁定文件、PostgreSQL 版本、事务隔离级别和回执模式。可下载夹具固定使用 Node.js 24.19.0、PostgreSQL 17.6 和 <code>pg</code> 8.23.1。</p> <p>对于提供的 Compose 设置，应准备一个 Docker 守护进程可正常工作的环境，并在发布之前验证 <code>docker info</code>。该数据库只接受公开的测试凭据，并绑定到本机回环接口。改编测试时，应使用应用实际采用的数据库引擎和相关设置。</p> <p>此示例假定 PostgreSQL 使用 READ COMMITTED、提供方事件 ID 稳定，并且回执保留时间覆盖提供方的重试/重放窗口。</p> <p>修正后的处理器会在同一个数据库事务中写入回执和订单。测试夹具会创建一次性数据库，并通过 <code>delivery_holds</code> 表模拟现有队列的隔离流程；该表仅属于测试基础设施，不是建议进行的应用模式迁移。</p> <p>应用修复不会新增队列、数据库服务、模式迁移或监控集成。如果这些假设不成立，应停止并重新界定范围。</p> <p><a id=\"run-the-sample\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_7\">运行示例</h3> <p>解压 ZIP，打开其目录，并通过 Compose 使用 Docker：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">docker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal up -d --wait postgres\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal run --rm runner node main.mjs baseline\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal run --rm runner node main.mjs regression\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal run --rm runner\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal down --volumes\n</code></pre> <p>基线命令必须以退出码 <code>1</code> 结束：重试断言观察到两笔订单和一条回执，而不是各一笔。回归命令会针对修正后的处理器运行相同断言，并必须以退出码 <code>0</code> 结束。</p> <p>最终测试命令会执行完整用例矩阵，包括独立连接、数据库锁竞争和旧数据孤立记录的隔离处理。</p> <p>如果你的安装使用 <code>docker-compose</code>，可用该命令拼写代替 <code>docker compose</code>。请按顺序运行这些命令；测试套件只会重置带有指定标记的测试模式。</p> <p><a id=\"inspect-the-transaction-handler\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_8\">检查事务处理器</h3> <p>以下片段来自下载包中的 <code>main.mjs</code>。解压后请运行完整文件；测试和夹具设置都包含在其中。</p> <details> <summary>回执申领与订单事务</summary> <pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">async function fixed(client, input, hook = async (_boundary) =&gt; {}) {\n  await assertNotHeld(client, input);\n  await hook(\"beforeClaim\");\n  await client.query(\"BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED\");\n  let committed = false;\n  try {\n    const claimed = await client.query(\n      `INSERT INTO event_receipts(provider, account_id, event_id, payload_fingerprint)\n       VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4)\n       ON CONFLICT (provider, account_id, event_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING event_id`,\n      [...key(input), fingerprint(input)]\n    );\n    trace(\"receipt-claim-result\", client, input, {\n      inserted: claimed.rowCount === 1,\n    });\n    if (claimed.rowCount === 1) {\n      await hook(\"afterClaim\");\n      await writeOrder(client, input);\n      await hook(\"afterOrder\");\n    } else {\n      // A new READ COMMITTED statement sees the winner after its commit.\n      await checkReceipt(client, input);\n    }\n    await client.query(\"COMMIT\");\n    committed = true;\n    trace(\"transaction-committed\", client, input);\n    await hook(\"afterCommit\");\n    trace(\"acknowledged\", client, input, acknowledgment);\n    return acknowledgment;\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (!committed) {\n      await client.query(\"ROLLBACK\");\n      trace(\"transaction-rolled-back-no-ack\", client, input);\n    } else {\n      trace(\"committed-response-lost-no-ack\", client, input);\n    }\n    throw error;\n  }\n}\n</code></pre> </details> <p><a id=\"1-provide-a-compact-packet\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"1\">1. 准备精简资料包</h3> <p>可按以下文件整理资料包：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">incidents/INC-DEMO-014/incident.md\nincidents/INC-DEMO-014/logs.ndjson\nincidents/INC-DEMO-014/release.json\nincidents/INC-DEMO-014/order-created.sanitized.json\n</code></pre> <p>在此场景中，<code>release.json</code> 标明示例发布版本的 SHA 为 <code>4a1c9e7d2b6f8a0c3d5e7f9012a4b6c8d0e2f413</code>，部署时间为 <code>2026-09-22T11:58:00Z</code>。事故时间范围是同一天的 <code>12:03:00Z–12:05:00Z</code>。资料包报告称，一个提供方事件对应两笔内部订单。准备自己的资料包时，应包含已确认的影响数量，并指出任何估算值。</p> <p>脱敏日志包含以下相互关联的观察记录：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>UTC 时间</th> <th>关联信息</th> <th>观察结果</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>12:03:04.110</td> <td>事件 <code>evt_demo_042</code>、账号 <code>acct_demo_01</code>、请求 <code>req_demo_a</code>、跟踪 <code>trace_demo_a</code></td> <td>第一次投递进入 Webhook 处理器。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:04.145</td> <td>同一事件和第一次跟踪</td> <td>订单 <code>ord_demo_101</code> 已提交。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:04.150</td> <td>同一工作进程</td> <td>在记录回执写入成功或响应之前，工作进程退出。仅凭此片段不能证明回执不存在。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:14.203</td> <td>同一提供方/账号/事件，请求 <code>req_demo_b</code>、跟踪 <code>trace_demo_b</code></td> <td>重试进入处理器。处理器记录称未找到匹配回执。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:14.241</td> <td>同一事件和第二次跟踪</td> <td>订单 <code>ord_demo_102</code> 已提交，并记录了一条回执。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>同一事件的多次投递应使用稳定的事件 ID，而每次投递都应使用不同的请求 ID 和跟踪 ID。若使用每个请求各自的标识符，就会掩盖重试与原始事件之间的关系。</p> <p><a id=\"2-trace-the-evidence-and-test-the-hypothesis\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"2\">2. 追踪证据并验证假设</h3> <p>事故报告提出的假设是：重试风暴导致了重复订单。Codex 应把它当作一种假设。观察到两次投递并不能证明发生了重试风暴；而重试本来就是 Webhook 处理器必须容忍的正常情况。</p> <p>检查示例中的受影响实现后，可以发现以下顺序：查询回执、提交订单，然后在另一个事务中写入回执。两次提交之间的间隙是更有力的解释。若工作进程在这段间隙中失败，订单可能已经提交而回执尚未写入，因此重试可能再创建一笔订单。并发投递也可能都在任何一方写入回执之前，通过各自独立的检查。</p> <p>让 Codex 追踪真实的处理器和事务边界，并引用相关代码行。它应确认资料包中的发布版本与受测代码一致；如果测试的是另一个提交版本，则要说明这会如何限制结论。</p> <p><a id=\"3-reproduce-before-changing-the-handler\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"3\">3. 修改处理器之前先复现</h3> <p>使用示例 Webhook 和隔离的 PostgreSQL 数据库添加回归测试。在原始处理器中，订单写入之后、回执写入之前加入一个仅供测试使用的故障钩子。</p> <p>根据真实事务边界确认此时原始订单已经提交。随后用相同的提供方/账号/事件重试，并断言最终恰好有一笔订单和一条匹配回执；植入缺陷的实现应因出现两笔订单而失败。</p> <p>修复之后，在订单语句执行后、共享事务提交之前触发对应的钩子；失败的尝试必须回滚为零笔订单、零条回执。</p> <p>然后再次重试，并断言两者各有一条。不同修订版本应使用相同的业务断言，并记录每个版本中的钩子位置。</p> <p>先在原始实现上运行新测试。此示例植入的缺陷预期会导致两笔订单，而不是一笔。保存命令、起始提交、退出码和断言输出。如果没有观察到该失败，应先调查差异，再修改处理器。</p> <p>注入异常可以测试应用的事务行为。应记录这并不能复现所有可能的主机、网络或数据库故障。</p> <p><a id=\"4-make-the-smallest-supported-fix\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"4\">4. 实施有依据的最小修复</h3> <p>对于这个特定缺陷，应在一个数据库连接上的单个事务中申领事件回执并写入订单。使用现有唯一键以及 <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (provider, account_id, event_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING ...</code>。</p> <p>只有成功插入回执的那次投递才能写入订单；只有事务提交之后才确认成功。如果事务失败，两项写入都会回滚。不要捕获唯一性错误后继续使用已中止的事务。</p> <p>竞争中的另一次投递可能需要等待第一个事务。如果第一个事务提交，第二次申领不会插入任何内容：在 READ COMMITTED 隔离级别下执行新的 SELECT，核实已存储事件的身份和载荷策略，然后返回现有的重复事件确认，不再写入订单。</p> <p>如果第一个事务回滚，竞争者就可以申领并处理该事件。跳过插入后却找不到回执、身份冲突或临时数据库错误，都必须按照已记录的重试/错误路径处理，不能伪造成功结果。</p> <p>在更严格的隔离级别下，序列化失败需要遵循代码库中有界的整笔事务重试策略。请参阅 PostgreSQL 的 <a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">INSERT 冲突行为（需联网）</a>和<a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">事务隔离（需联网）</a>。</p> <p>无论是进程内集合，还是先单独“检查再插入”，都无法防止并发工作进程或服务重启。事件键的范围应符合提供方对事件身份的约定。如果一个看似相同的事件携带了冲突的身份或载荷信息，应明确报告这种不一致，而不是悄悄把它当作有效重复事件。</p> <p>示例中的业务变更完全发生在同一个数据库里。如果真实处理器还会发送电子邮件、扣款或调用其他服务，仅靠这个事务并不能让这些外部副作用具备幂等性。扩大修复范围之前，应先确认该边界以及系统现有的投递或幂等机制。</p> <p><a id=\"5-verify-the-changed-behavior\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"5\">5. 验证行为变化</h3> <p>同时要求提供原始失败和修复后的结果。在明确标记的事务边界使用仅供测试的钩子，并通过另一个连接检查已提交状态。示例通过了 11 个本地 PostgreSQL 测试；下载包中的 <code>test-evidence.md</code> 记录了这些结果，以及仍需在 Cloud 环境和部署中完成的检查。在依赖该恢复路径之前，应在已发布环境中运行套件，并测试提交期间实际发生连接中断的情况。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>检查/故障点</th> <th>预期行为</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>首次投递和已提交的重复事件</td> <td>第一次投递创建一笔订单和一条回执；重复事件返回约定的确认，并且数量不变。记录准确的状态码/正文。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>申领回执之前</td> <td>故障不得创建任何记录，也不得返回成功确认。重试应创建各一条记录。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>已申领回执、尚未写入订单</td> <td>故障应回滚回执；订单不存在。重试可以正常申领并处理事件。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>已写入订单、尚未提交</td> <td>故障应回滚两项记录。这是修复后代码中与原始“订单已提交但回执缺失”缺陷相对应的故障点；重试后应各有一条记录。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>已提交、尚未确认</td> <td>模拟响应丢失。两项记录均保持已提交。重试应返回重复事件确认，且不再创建订单。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>提交结果未知</td> <td>单独模拟提交附近的连接中断，不要与普通异常混为一谈。不得作出没有依据的成功声明；使用同一个稳定事件键重试时，无论第一次事务已经提交还是已回滚，都必须安全。如果未测试此传输故障情况，应将其标记为未运行。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>并发重复事件：第一个事务提交</td> <td>使用两个独立连接/工作进程竞争同一事件键。第一个事务在申领后暂停；在释放它之前启动竞争者。确认竞争者确实发生等待/竞争，再释放第一个事务。两者都应通过预期的确认路径完成；最终恰好有一笔订单和一条回执。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>并发重复事件：第一个事务回滚</td> <td>重复执行受控并发，但在提交前让申领者失败。回滚后，竞争者申领事件并创建一笔订单和一条回执。检查两个响应和最终状态。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>事件身份和载荷冲突</td> <td>不同事件以及不同账号/提供方下的同一事件 ID 应相互独立。若同一范围内的 ID 对应冲突的规范化载荷，应遵循约定的冲突处理路径；不得创建第二笔订单，也不得悄悄确认不同的工作。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>缺少回执且仍有待重试的旧订单</td> <td>植入一笔已提交但没有回执的旧订单。在恢复重试之前识别并暂扣该事件键。暂扣中的重试不得返回成功确认或创建新记录。基于可信证据完成对账后，可以返回重复事件确认，同时保持现有订单数量为一；证据缺失或冲突时，事件键必须继续暂扣。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>更广泛的服务检查</td> <td>运行相关集成测试、lint 和类型检查。分别说明新失败、既有失败，以及无法运行的检查。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>使用屏障/锁存器和有界超时协调并发，不要依赖 sleep 或仅仅启动两个 Promise。应在测试清理阶段释放被阻塞的工作进程，并使用一次性夹具。仅测试异常并不能证明已覆盖进程突然退出、传输丢失或生产环境并发行为。</p> <p>示例在固定的运行器中使用 <code>node main.mjs baseline</code>、<code>node main.mjs regression</code> 和 <code>node main.mjs</code>。其结构化输出会记录事务边界、已提交行数、确认状态码/正文和观察到的阻塞后端 ID。</p> <p>改编工作流时，应使用代码库实际定义的命令并报告真实结果。跳过的检查或不可用的依赖项应在交接材料中说明，并指出它们对结论可信度的影响。</p> <p><a id=\"review-the-handoff\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">审核交接材料</h2> <p><a id=\"shared-evidence-record\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_10\">共享证据记录</h3> <p>对每项验收标准使用事故工作流的证据格式。根据实际观察到的输出填写；下面的字段只是模板，不代表已经通过。如果新测试在基线中尚不存在，应将“尚未添加”与现有检查失败区分开来。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Criterion / test: [specific behavior and test location]\nEnvironment: [published setup reference; runtime, database and dependency versions]\nBefore: [revision + test-only diff ID; command; exit code; observed assertion; log path]\nAfter: [final revision or base + diff ID; command; exit code; observed assertion; log path]\nStatus: [pass / fail / blocked / not run]\nLimits / reviewer action: [untested behavior, blocker, or decision required]\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>模板中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">验收标准 / 测试：[specific behavior and test location]\n环境：[published setup reference; runtime, database and dependency versions]\n修改前：[revision + test-only diff ID; command; exit code; observed assertion; log path]\n修改后：[final revision or base + diff ID; command; exit code; observed assertion; log path]\n状态：[pass / fail / blocked / not run]\n局限 / 审阅者需处理的事项：[untested behavior, blocker, or decision required]</div></div> <p>应将红灯和绿灯运行分别绑定到实际测试过的状态，包括在原始修订上添加的仅供测试的更改。日志、响应夹具和最终差异都应与交接材料放在一起。如果最终修订发生变化，就应更新证据；未运行或受阻的检查不能算作通过。</p> <p>请让 Codex 将代码差异和以下审核材料留在代码库中：</p> <ul> <li><code>incident-investigation.md</code>：时间线、证据引用、已测试的假设、有依据支持的原因以及仍未消除的不确定性。</li> <li><code>test-evidence.md</code>：基线提交、失败的回归测试输出、修复提交或差异、通过的目标检查、更广泛的检查，以及各项限制。</li> <li><code>pr-draft.md</code>：对缺陷、行为变化、测试证据和剩余风险的简洁说明。</li> <li><code>deployment-handoff.md</code>：已验证的模式/隔离级别/回执保留假设、具名发布负责人、部署前检查、重复订单和确认/错误指标，以及以下发布/回滚门槛。</li> </ul> <p>接受补丁之前，应核实原始代码会让新测试失败、修复后测试通过，并且差异确实解决了已证明的缺陷。在 Webhook 示例中，应检查事件键范围、事务边界、并发覆盖，以及事务之外的任何副作用。还要单独审核现有重复订单；防止新增重复订单并不能修复历史记录。</p> <p><a id=\"plan-deployment-and-rollback\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">规划部署和回滚</h3> <p>代码补丁只保护运行修正后处理器的工作进程。旧工作进程仍可能在申领回执之前写入订单，因此混合版本的金丝雀部署不能证明缺陷已经消除。</p> <p>部署负责人必须选择以下其中一种方案：经过测试的混合版本策略；或使用现有操作手册暂停接收、排空正在运行的旧工作进程、更新所有消费者，然后无损恢复处理保留事件的流程。</p> <p>选择后一种方案之前，应确认提供方/队列的重试与保留行为；不要假设暂停就一定不会丢失事件。</p> <p>恢复处理保留事件之前，应找出没有匹配回执的已提交历史订单。暂停并排空旧消费者可以防止继续产生新的旧版写入，但不能修复已经存在的订单。使用现有队列或隔离流程暂扣每一个受影响的事件键。如果旧记录无法确定完整的事件身份，应继续暂停受影响投递或相应时间范围的投递，交由负责人审核，不要声称扫描已经完成。</p> <p>只有在可信、不可变的提供方/账号/事件身份、规范化载荷证据和已提交业务记录能够说明已发生的事情时，才能对被暂扣的事件键进行对账。</p> <p>根据批准的对账流程记录匹配回执，但不要重复执行业务变更；随后解除该事件键的暂扣。</p> <p>如果证据缺失、载荷冲突或存在多条业务记录，应继续隔离相关事件键，交由负责人审核。重试载荷不能取代原始事件的可信证据，不能用来重建其指纹。</p> <p>只有在每个受影响的事件键都已完成对账，或其暂扣状态仍能在所有消费者中持续生效时，恢复处理前的门槛才算通过。</p> <p>在预发布环境中植入一笔旧订单及其待重试事件。验证暂扣机制能够阻止创建另一笔订单和发送成功确认；完成可信对账后，重试可以收到重复事件确认，而现有订单数量仍为一。还要演练证据无法解决的情况：事件必须继续暂扣，不能伪造回执。将此门槛连同暂停/排空、消费者版本、保留期限和回滚检查一起记录在部署交接材料中。</p> <p>发布之前，应确认现有唯一约束存在且有效、所有消费者的回执键/载荷策略一致、清理回执不会重新打开重放窗口，并确认外部副作用不在此补丁的处理范围内。</p> <p>在预发布环境中演练选定的处理顺序以及工作进程失败后的重试。记录负责人的观察窗口，以及重复订单、Webhook 错误、确认延迟、锁等待和重试积压的停止阈值；不要自行编造生产阈值。</p> <p>回滚到旧处理器可能会再次引入重复订单。回滚交接材料必须写明隔离措施（例如获准的暂停/排空流程）、安全的代码修订版本，以及决定何时恢复处理的负责人。保留回执；不要为了让重试继续而删除回执。历史重复订单需要单独审核和修复。</p> <p>工程师负责部署决定和任何生产修复。隔离测试通过只是审核证据，并不能证明生产环境健康。创建拉取请求，运行团队的常规 CI 和审核流程，并在审核草稿之后，通过团队既定控制流程部署。</p> <p><a id=\"continue-in-the-same-task\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_12\">在同一任务中继续</h2> <p>使用聚焦的后续提示来解决不确定性或完善交接材料：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Review the patch against two concurrent deliveries and a retry after a worker failure. Show which behaviors the tests establish and which remain untested. Keep the change limited to this handler and its regression tests; update the handoff with any remaining risk.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">针对两次并发投递，以及工作进程失败后的重试，审查这个补丁。说明测试证明了哪些行为，以及哪些行为仍未测试。将更改限制在这个处理程序及其回归测试内，并在交接材料中补充任何剩余风险。</div></div> <p>如果第一次运行未能复现事故：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Stop implementation. List the hypotheses the evidence rules out, the strongest remaining hypothesis, and the smallest additional sanitized input or isolated experiment needed to distinguish it. Keep the root cause unconfirmed.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">停止实现。列出证据已排除的假设、剩余假设中最有力的一项，以及区分该假设所需的最小额外脱敏输入或隔离实验。继续将根因标为未确认。</div></div>","en":"<h1 id=\"investigate-an-incident-and-test-a-fix\">Investigate an incident and test a fix</h1> <p>Turn an incident packet into a reproduced bug, a focused patch, and a reviewable handoff.</p> <p>Give Codex Cloud a sanitized incident packet and a working test environment. Investigate the evidence, reproduce a supported code defect, and return a focused patch with regression-test evidence and a deployment and rollback handoff.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Small engineering teams investigating a stabilized incident.</li> <li>Application defects with sanitized evidence and safe reproduction inputs.</li> <li>Teams reviewing a focused fix in a working, isolated test environment.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Investigate an incident</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Investigate [incident ID] in [repository/service] using [path to sanitized incident packet]. Work in the published cloud environment selected for this task.\n\nThe packet identifies the incident time window, affected release SHA, expected behavior, observed behavior, relevant log and trace excerpts, and safe reproduction inputs. Read AGENTS.md and the repository's setup and testing instructions first. Check that the checkout matches the affected release, or explain the difference before drawing conclusions.\n\nBuild a short timeline with file and line references to the packet and source code. Separate observations, hypotheses, and evidence gaps. Test the strongest hypothesis in the isolated test environment. If you cannot reproduce the failure or lack decisive evidence, return an investigation with the next diagnostic step; do not claim a confirmed root cause or make a speculative fix.\n\nIf the evidence supports a code defect, add a regression test and run it against the original code to demonstrate the failure. Make the smallest fix, rerun that test, then run the relevant broader tests, lint, and type checks available in this repository. Record the commands, exit codes, and any checks you could not run.\n\nPrepare a branch diff, a draft pull-request description, test-evidence.md, and a deployment and rollback handoff. Include the supported cause, unresolved uncertainty, and behavior changed. Use one evidence row per criterion: revision and environment versions, baseline command/exit/log, final command/exit/log, actual result, and pass/fail/blocked/not-run status with limitations. Keep the PR description as a local draft for review.\n\nFor this duplicate-webhook example, name the before-commit and after-commit failure points, prove duplicate contention with independent database connections, and state isolation, receipt-retention, and mixed-version rollout assumptions.\n\nFor this duplicate-webhook example, before proposing that retained deliveries resume, identify legacy orders without matching receipts after old consumers have drained. Hold affected event keys using the existing queue or quarantine procedure. Add a seeded orphan-with-pending-retry test that proves no second order or success acknowledgment occurs while held. Reconcile only from trusted immutable event identity, canonical payload, and committed-order evidence; keep missing or conflicting evidence quarantined for owner review. Do not invent fingerprints, delete receipts, or replay an unresolved legacy event.\n\nUse only the supplied packet and repository unless I identify another approved source. Access only the explicitly approved read-only monitoring source, if supplied. Write code and task artifacts only in this repository; use only its isolated test database. Do not connect directly to production application or database hosts, or modify production systems. Do not replay production events, modify live data or monitoring, create or post external updates, merge, or deploy.\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">Cloud environments</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Give Codex one bounded incident to investigate in a prepared cloud environment. The goal is a result an engineer can review: a supported explanation, a regression test that fails before the fix, a focused patch, and evidence from the tests that ran.</p> <p>This works well after the immediate incident has been stabilized and the remaining job is to understand and fix an application defect. If the available evidence cannot establish the cause, a useful result is a clear account of the investigation and the next diagnostic step.</p> <h2 id=\"prepare-the-environment-and-incident-packet\">Prepare the environment and incident packet</h2> <ol> <li> <p>In the web or desktop app, choose <strong>Work in</strong> &gt; <strong>Cloud</strong> and select a published environment.</p> </li> <li> <p>To create one, select <strong>Create environment</strong>, choose the GitHub repositories, and ask Codex to prepare the runtime, dependencies, and isolated test services.</p> </li> <li> <p>Review the setup report, resolve failed checks, and <strong>Publish</strong> before starting a new task.</p> </li> <li> <p>Follow the <a href=\"#/article/a-7ac5ae1c18e03bf1\">current Cloud environment guide</a>.</p> </li> <li>Keep repository guidance in <a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">AGENTS.md</a>.</li> </ol> <p>If dependencies change or service setup changes, edit the reusable environment, test it, and <strong>Republish</strong>; then validate the updated environment in a new task.</p> <p>Repository refresh does not rerun install/start commands, and existing tasks retain their own state.</p> <p>Configure required network destinations and test credentials separately; allowing a destination does not grant service access.</p> <p>Confirm that the affected checkout, dependencies, checks, and disposable test database work in the new task. Record the starting commit and test baseline.</p> <p>Keep the initial investigation self-contained. Place a sanitized packet in the repository or make its files available to the task. An engineer should assemble and check it before starting:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Input</th> <th>What to include</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Incident summary</td> <td>Incident ID, UTC time window, expected and observed behavior, known impact, and current status. Distinguish confirmed counts from estimates.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Release identity</td> <td>The deployed commit SHA, deployment time, relevant configuration values without secrets, and the last known good release if available.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Correlated evidence</td> <td>Log and trace excerpts with timestamps, event IDs, request IDs, trace IDs, and file references. Preserve identifiers consistently when sanitizing.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Safe reproduction</td> <td>Test request bodies, relevant fixtures, and instructions for exercising the suspected path without calling live services.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Repository guidance</td> <td>Relevant paths, database engine, test commands, permitted changes, and the engineer who will review the result.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Do not include access tokens, customer payloads, or personal data in the packet.</p> <p>A monitoring integration is optional. If you use one, grant read-only access to the relevant project and time window; the file-based workflow does not require it. Repository instructions should keep test credentials separate from production configuration.</p> <h2 id=\"run-the-starter-prompt\">Run the starter prompt</h2> <p>Use the starter prompt on this page and replace its placeholders with your incident ID, repository, and packet path. The first result should connect the evidence to the affected code, explain what can be reproduced, and identify missing information.</p> <p>Ask Codex to continue through implementation only when the evidence supports a code defect. Otherwise, review the investigation and provide the missing input. A plausible explanation, a recent code change, or a successful test on a different release is not enough to establish the cause.</p> <h2 id=\"example-a-retried-webhook-creates-a-duplicate-order\">Example: A retried webhook creates a duplicate order</h2> <p>Use the <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/use-cases/incident-to-tested-fix/sample.zip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">runnable incident sample (online)</a> to practice the investigation. It includes a prepared incident packet, a handler with a reproducible defect, the corrected handler, and PostgreSQL tests.</p> <p>The service receives an <code>order.created</code> webhook, writes an order, and records that the event has been processed. During an incident, a retry appears to create a second order. The team has already stabilized the service and wants a patch for review.</p> <p>The sample uses JavaScript, Node.js's test runner, and PostgreSQL. Its disposable fixture has an <code>event_receipts</code> table with non-null identity columns and a non-deferrable unique key on <code>(provider, account_id, event_id)</code>.</p> <p>The schema permits a receipt claim before the order exists and retains the canonical payload fingerprint needed to detect conflicting reuse of the same event key.</p> <p>If your application instead requires an order foreign key at claim time or lacks this conflict evidence, stop for a separate schema/design decision.</p> <p>Record the exact runtime, dependency lock, PostgreSQL version, transaction isolation level, and receipt schema in the evidence. The downloadable fixture pins Node.js 24.19.0, PostgreSQL 17.6, and <code>pg</code> 8.23.1.</p> <p>For the supplied Compose setup, prepare an environment with a working Docker daemon and verify <code>docker info</code> before publishing it. Its database accepts only public test credentials and is bound to the local loopback interface. Use your application's database engine and relevant settings when adapting the test.</p> <p>This example assumes PostgreSQL READ COMMITTED, stable provider event IDs, and receipt retention covering the provider's retry/replay window.</p> <p>The fixed handler places receipt and order writes in one database transaction. The fixture provisions a disposable database and a <code>delivery_holds</code> table to model an existing queue's quarantine procedure; that table is test infrastructure, not a proposed application migration.</p> <p>The application fix adds no queue, database service, schema migration, or monitoring integration. If those assumptions do not hold, stop and revise the scope.</p> <h3 id=\"run-the-sample\">Run the sample</h3> <p>Extract the ZIP, open its directory, and use Docker with Compose:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">docker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal up -d --wait postgres\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal run --rm runner node main.mjs baseline\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal run --rm runner node main.mjs regression\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal run --rm runner\ndocker compose -p codex-incident-rehearsal down --volumes\n</code></pre> <p>The baseline command must exit with code <code>1</code>: the retry assertion observes two orders and one receipt instead of one of each. The regression command runs the same assertion against the corrected handler and must exit with code <code>0</code>.</p> <p>The final test command exercises the full matrix, including independent connections, database lock contention, and legacy-orphan quarantine.</p> <p>If your installation provides <code>docker-compose</code>, use that spelling in place of <code>docker compose</code>. Run these commands sequentially; the suite resets only its marked test schema.</p> <h3 id=\"inspect-the-transaction-handler\">Inspect the transaction handler</h3> <p>This excerpt comes from <code>main.mjs</code> in the download. Run the complete files after extracting the ZIP; the tests and fixture setup are included there.</p> <details> <summary>Receipt claim and order transaction</summary> <pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">async function fixed(client, input, hook = async (_boundary) =&gt; {}) {\n  await assertNotHeld(client, input);\n  await hook(\"beforeClaim\");\n  await client.query(\"BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED\");\n  let committed = false;\n  try {\n    const claimed = await client.query(\n      `INSERT INTO event_receipts(provider, account_id, event_id, payload_fingerprint)\n       VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4)\n       ON CONFLICT (provider, account_id, event_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING event_id`,\n      [...key(input), fingerprint(input)]\n    );\n    trace(\"receipt-claim-result\", client, input, {\n      inserted: claimed.rowCount === 1,\n    });\n    if (claimed.rowCount === 1) {\n      await hook(\"afterClaim\");\n      await writeOrder(client, input);\n      await hook(\"afterOrder\");\n    } else {\n      // A new READ COMMITTED statement sees the winner after its commit.\n      await checkReceipt(client, input);\n    }\n    await client.query(\"COMMIT\");\n    committed = true;\n    trace(\"transaction-committed\", client, input);\n    await hook(\"afterCommit\");\n    trace(\"acknowledged\", client, input, acknowledgment);\n    return acknowledgment;\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (!committed) {\n      await client.query(\"ROLLBACK\");\n      trace(\"transaction-rolled-back-no-ack\", client, input);\n    } else {\n      trace(\"committed-response-lost-no-ack\", client, input);\n    }\n    throw error;\n  }\n}\n</code></pre> </details> <h3 id=\"1-provide-a-compact-packet\">1. Provide a compact packet</h3> <p>Use these files as the packet outline:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">incidents/INC-DEMO-014/incident.md\nincidents/INC-DEMO-014/logs.ndjson\nincidents/INC-DEMO-014/release.json\nincidents/INC-DEMO-014/order-created.sanitized.json\n</code></pre> <p>In this scenario, <code>release.json</code> identifies the example release SHA <code>4a1c9e7d2b6f8a0c3d5e7f9012a4b6c8d0e2f413</code>, deployed at <code>2026-09-22T11:58:00Z</code>. The incident window is <code>12:03:00Z–12:05:00Z</code> on the same day. The packet reports two internal orders associated with one provider event. When preparing your own packet, include confirmed impact counts and identify any estimates.</p> <p>The sanitized logs contain these correlated observations:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>UTC time</th> <th>Correlation</th> <th>Observation</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>12:03:04.110</td> <td>Event <code>evt_demo_042</code>, account <code>acct_demo_01</code>, request <code>req_demo_a</code>, trace <code>trace_demo_a</code></td> <td>First delivery enters the webhook handler.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:04.145</td> <td>Same event and first trace</td> <td>Order <code>ord_demo_101</code> is committed.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:04.150</td> <td>Same worker</td> <td>Worker exits before a receipt-write success or response is recorded. The excerpt alone does not prove the receipt is absent.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:14.203</td> <td>Same provider/account/event, request <code>req_demo_b</code>, trace <code>trace_demo_b</code></td> <td>A retry enters the handler. The handler logs that no matching receipt was found.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>12:03:14.241</td> <td>Same event and second trace</td> <td>Order <code>ord_demo_102</code> is committed and a receipt is recorded.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Keep a stable event ID across deliveries and distinct request and trace IDs per delivery. A per-request identifier would hide the relationship between the retry and the original event.</p> <h3 id=\"2-trace-the-evidence-and-test-the-hypothesis\">2. Trace the evidence and test the hypothesis</h3> <p>The incident report proposes that a retry storm caused the duplicate. Codex should treat that as a hypothesis. Two observed deliveries do not establish a storm, and retries are a normal condition a webhook handler must tolerate.</p> <p>In the sample’s affected implementation, source inspection reveals this sequence: look up a receipt, commit the order, then write the receipt in a separate transaction. The gap between the two commits is a stronger explanation. A worker failure in that gap can leave an order without a receipt, so a retry can create another order. Concurrent deliveries can also both pass a separate check before either writes its receipt.</p> <p>Have Codex trace the actual handler and transaction boundaries and cite the relevant lines. It should confirm the packet's release matches the code under test, or explain why testing a different commit limits the conclusion.</p> <h3 id=\"3-reproduce-before-changing-the-handler\">3. Reproduce before changing the handler</h3> <p>Add a regression test using the sample webhook and the isolated PostgreSQL database. Put a test-only failure hook immediately after the order write and before the receipt-write step in the original handler.</p> <p>Confirm from the real transaction boundaries that the original order has committed at that point. Retry the same provider/account/event and assert exactly one order and one matching receipt; the seeded implementation should fail with two orders.</p> <p>After the fix, exercise the corresponding hook after the order statement but before the shared transaction commits; the failed attempt must leave zero orders and zero receipts.</p> <p>Retry and assert one of each. Keep the same business assertion across revisions and record the hook location on each revision.</p> <p>Run the new test on the original implementation first. For the seeded defect in this sample, the expected failure is two orders instead of one. Save the command, starting commit, exit code, and assertion output. If that failure does not occur, investigate the mismatch before editing the handler.</p> <p>An injected exception exercises the application's transaction behavior. Record that it does not reproduce every possible host, network, or database failure.</p> <h3 id=\"4-make-the-smallest-supported-fix\">4. Make the smallest supported fix</h3> <p>For this specific defect, claim the event receipt and write the order within one transaction on one database connection. Use the existing unique key with <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (provider, account_id, event_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING ...</code>.</p> <p>Only the delivery that inserts the receipt may write the order; acknowledge success only after commit. A failed transaction rolls back both writes. Do not catch a uniqueness error and continue using an aborted transaction.</p> <p>A competing delivery may wait for the first transaction. If the first commits, the second claim inserts nothing: issue a fresh SELECT under READ COMMITTED, verify the stored event identity/payload policy, and return the existing duplicate acknowledgment without writing an order.</p> <p>If the first transaction rolls back, the competitor can claim and process the event. A missing receipt after a skipped insert, an identity conflict, or a transient database error must follow the documented retry/error path, not a fabricated success.</p> <p>Under stricter isolation, serialization failures require the repository's bounded whole-transaction retry policy. See PostgreSQL's <a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">INSERT conflict behavior (online)</a> and <a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">transaction isolation (online)</a>.</p> <p>Neither an in-memory set nor a separate “check then insert” protects against concurrent workers or restarts. Scope the key to the provider's event-identity contract. If a supposedly identical event carries conflicting identity or payload information, surface that inconsistency rather than silently treating it as a valid duplicate.</p> <p>The demo's business mutation is entirely within the same database. If the real handler also sends email, charges a card, or calls another service, this transaction alone does not make those external effects idempotent. Identify that boundary and the system's existing delivery or idempotency mechanism before expanding the fix.</p> <h3 id=\"5-verify-the-changed-behavior\">5. Verify the changed behavior</h3> <p>Ask for both the original failure and the post-fix result. Use test-only hooks at named transaction boundaries and inspect committed state from a separate connection. The sample passes eleven local PostgreSQL tests; <code>test-evidence.md</code> in the download records those results and the checks still needed for Cloud execution and deployment. Run the suite in your published environment and test actual connection loss during commit before relying on that recovery path.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Check / failure point</th> <th>Required behavior</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>First delivery and committed duplicate</td> <td>The first creates one order and one receipt; a repeated event returns the agreed acknowledgment and leaves counts unchanged. Record exact status/body.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Before receipt claim</td> <td>Failure creates neither row and returns no success acknowledgment. A retry creates one of each.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>After claim, before order write</td> <td>Failure rolls back the receipt; no order exists. A retry can claim and process normally.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>After order write, before commit</td> <td>Failure rolls back both rows. This is the fixed-code counterpart of the original committed-order/missing-receipt defect; a retry leaves one of each.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>After commit, before acknowledgment</td> <td>Simulate a lost response. Both rows remain committed. A retry returns the duplicate acknowledgment without another order.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Commit outcome unknown</td> <td>Model a connection loss around commit separately from an ordinary exception. Return no unsupported success claim; a retry with the same stable event key must be safe whether the first transaction committed or rolled back. If this transport case was not exercised, mark it not run.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Concurrent duplicate: first transaction commits</td> <td>Two independent connections/workers contend on the same event key. Hold the winner after claim; start the competitor before releasing it. Verify the competitor actually contends, then release the winner. Both complete through the expected acknowledgment path; exactly one order and one receipt exist.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Concurrent duplicate: first transaction rolls back</td> <td>Repeat the controlled overlap, but fail the claimant before commit. The competitor claims after rollback and creates one order and one receipt. Check both responses and final state.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Event identity and payload conflict</td> <td>Different events and the same event ID under different accounts/providers remain independent. For an event with the same scoped ID but a conflicting canonical payload, follow the agreed conflict path; do not create a second order or silently acknowledge different work.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Legacy order without receipt and pending retry</td> <td>Seed a committed legacy order without a receipt. Identify and hold its event key before retries resume. A held retry returns no success acknowledgment and creates no row. Trusted reconciliation permits the duplicate acknowledgment with one existing order and one receipt; missing or conflicting evidence keeps the key held.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Broader service checks</td> <td>Run relevant integration tests, lint, and type checking. Separate new failures, pre-existing failures, and checks that could not run.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Coordinate concurrency with barriers/latches and bounded timeouts, not sleeps or a bare pair of promises. Release blocked workers in test cleanup and use disposable fixtures. An exception test alone does not prove abrupt process death, transport loss, or production concurrency behavior.</p> <p>The sample uses <code>node main.mjs baseline</code>, <code>node main.mjs regression</code>, and <code>node main.mjs</code> inside the pinned runner. Its structured output records transaction boundaries, committed row counts, acknowledgment status/body, and observed blocking backend IDs.</p> <p>When adapting the workflow, use the commands actually defined by your repository and report their real results. A skipped check or unavailable dependency belongs in the handoff, with its impact on confidence.</p> <h2 id=\"review-the-handoff\">Review the handoff</h2> <h3 id=\"shared-evidence-record\">Shared evidence record</h3> <p>Use the incident workflow's evidence format for each acceptance criterion. Populate it from observed output; the fields below are a template, not passing results. For a new test that does not exist at baseline, distinguish “not yet present” from a failed existing check.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Criterion / test: [specific behavior and test location]\nEnvironment: [published setup reference; runtime, database and dependency versions]\nBefore: [revision + test-only diff ID; command; exit code; observed assertion; log path]\nAfter: [final revision or base + diff ID; command; exit code; observed assertion; log path]\nStatus: [pass / fail / blocked / not run]\nLimits / reviewer action: [untested behavior, blocker, or decision required]\n</code></pre> <p>Keep red and green runs tied to their actual tested states, including test-only changes on the original revision. Keep the logs, response fixtures, and final diff with the handoff. A changed final revision needs updated evidence; checks that were not run or were blocked cannot count as passing.</p> <p>Ask Codex to leave the code diff and these review artifacts in the repository:</p> <ul> <li><code>incident-investigation.md</code>: timeline, evidence references, hypotheses tested, supported cause, and remaining uncertainty.</li> <li><code>test-evidence.md</code>: baseline commit, failing regression output, fix commit or diff, passing targeted checks, broader checks, and limitations.</li> <li><code>pr-draft.md</code>: a concise explanation of the defect, changed behavior, test evidence, and remaining risk.</li> <li><code>deployment-handoff.md</code>: verified schema/isolation/receipt-retention assumptions, named rollout owner, pre-deployment checks, duplicate-order and acknowledgment/error metrics, and the rollout/rollback gates below.</li> </ul> <p>Before accepting the patch, verify that the original code fails the new test, the fix passes it, and the diff addresses the demonstrated defect. In the webhook example, inspect event-key scope, transaction boundaries, concurrency coverage, and any effects outside the transaction. Review existing duplicate orders separately; preventing new duplicates does not repair historical records.</p> <h3 id=\"plan-deployment-and-rollback\">Plan deployment and rollback</h3> <p>A code patch protects only workers running the corrected handler. Old workers can still write an order before claiming a receipt, so a mixed-version canary is not evidence that the defect is eliminated.</p> <p>The deployment owner must choose either a tested mixed-version strategy or an existing runbook that pauses ingestion, drains in-flight old workers, updates every consumer, and resumes retained deliveries without loss.</p> <p>Confirm the provider/queue retry and retention behavior before choosing the latter; do not assume pausing is lossless.</p> <p>Before resuming retained deliveries, identify committed legacy orders with no matching receipt. Pausing and draining old consumers prevents new legacy writes; it does not repair those existing orders. Hold every affected event key with the existing queue or quarantine procedure. If legacy records cannot establish a complete event identity, keep the affected deliveries or those from the affected time window paused for owner review rather than declaring the scan complete.</p> <p>Reconcile a held key only when trusted immutable provider/account/event identity, canonical payload evidence, and the committed business record establish what already happened.</p> <p>Under the approved reconciliation procedure, record the matching receipt without repeating the business mutation, then release that key's hold.</p> <p>Keep affected keys quarantined for owner review when evidence is missing, payloads conflict, or multiple business records exist. A retry payload cannot substitute for trusted evidence of the original event when reconstructing its fingerprint.</p> <p>The pre-resume gate passes only when each affected key is reconciled or its hold remains enforced across all consumers.</p> <p>Seed a legacy order and its pending retry in staging. Verify the hold prevents another order and any success acknowledgment; after trusted reconciliation, the retry may receive the duplicate acknowledgment while the existing order count stays at one. Rehearse an unresolved case too: it must remain held without a fabricated receipt. Keep this gate in the deployment handoff alongside pause/drain, consumer-version, retention, and rollback checks.</p> <p>Before rollout, verify the existing unique constraint is present and valid, the receipt key/payload policy is consistent across all consumers, receipt cleanup cannot reopen the replay window, and external effects remain outside this patch.</p> <p>In staging, rehearse the selected sequence and failed-worker retry. Record the owner's observation window and stop thresholds for duplicate orders, webhook errors, acknowledgment latency, lock waits, and retry backlog; do not invent production thresholds.</p> <p>Reverting to the old handler can reintroduce duplicate orders. The rollback handoff must name the containment step (for example, the approved pause/drain procedure), the safe revision, and the owner who decides when processing can resume. Preserve receipts; do not delete them to unblock retries. Historical duplicate orders require a separately reviewed remediation.</p> <p>The engineer owns the deployment decision and any production remediation. A passing isolated test is evidence for review, not proof that production is healthy. Create the pull request, run your normal CI and review process, and deploy through your team's established controls after reviewing the draft.</p> <h2 id=\"continue-in-the-same-task\">Continue in the same task</h2> <p>Use a focused follow-up to resolve uncertainty or improve the handoff:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Review the patch against two concurrent deliveries and a retry after a worker failure. Show which behaviors the tests establish and which remain untested. Keep the change limited to this handler and its regression tests; update the handoff with any remaining risk.\n</code></pre> <p>If the first run could not reproduce the incident:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Stop implementation. List the hypotheses the evidence rules out, the strongest remaining hypothesis, and the smallest additional sanitized input or isolated experiment needed to distinguish it. Keep the root cause unconfirmed.\n</code></pre>"};
